title: That Ye Might Believe: The Gospel of John
description: A chapter-by-chapter study of the Gospel of John: the Johannine misunderstanding motif, the seven “I am” figures and the bare “I am,” Semitic parallelism, and the one plain truth they all point to: believe on the Son.
output: lessons/bible/john-that-ye-might-believe.html
highlight: believ

# Rebuilt from the author's revised outline, "John: That Ye Might Believe — Chapter-by-Chapter Outline" (Nov 13, 2026),
# point for point, with no break between nights. Slide text is the outline's own wording; verses are pulled from the KJV.
# Layout: the two devices first (the misunderstanding motif with the seven "I am" figures and the bare "I am", then
# Semitic parallelism), then John 1-21. Each chapter slide is kicker "John N" -> the outline's Setting as the lead ->
# fig:/lit:/rev: cards for the figure, the literal hearer and the meaning -> the outline's Note as the take.
# Fixed: the second parallelism heading was numbered "1." (now "2."); a doubled space in the John 7 note.

=== Introduction
--- That Ye Might Believe [title]
overline: The Gospel of John
sub: Chapter by chapter through the figures, dark sayings and paired lines John uses to point every reader to one plain truth: believe on the Son.
refs: John 20:31

--- Written that ye might believe
big: John 20:31
- Because of this, we often recommend to new Christians to read John first.
- The purpose John has informs the format he used specifically.

--- Two literary devices to watch for
lead: John wraps the teachings of Jesus in two recurring devices. Both point the reader to the same plain truth: believe on the Son ([[John 20:31]]).
panel: 1. The Johannine Misunderstanding Motif | Jesus speaks a spiritual truth in a figure, a carnal hearer takes it literally, and the plain meaning is given.
panel: 2. Semitic Parallelism | John pairs lines the way Hebrew writers did. The second line restates or contrasts the first.

=== Device 1: The Misunderstanding Motif
--- The Johannine Misunderstanding Motif
kicker: Device 1
lead: Jesus speaks a spiritual truth in a parable, figure or “dark saying,” often uses a word with a double meaning (*amphibologia*). A carnal hearer takes it literally and physically. Jesus, or John, then uses their misunderstanding to explain the spiritual meaning.
fig: The figure |  | A picture Jesus uses: temple, birth, water, bread, door, sleep.
lit: The literal hearer |  | Someone takes it at face value and is confused or offended.
rev: The revealed meaning |  | The plain truth, most often a call to believe on Him.

--- The Johannine Misunderstanding Motif, continued
kicker: Device 1
big: John 16:25

--- The Seven “I Am” Figures
kicker: Device 1 in action
lead: Jesus uses this motif to declare His divinity in a picture, and each carries the gospel inside it: **believe on Him.**
tile: [[John 6:35]] | The bread of life
tile: [[John 8:12]] | The light of the world
tile: [[John 10:9]] | The door
tile: [[John 10:11]] | The good shepherd
tile: [[John 11:25-26]] | The resurrection, and the life
tile: [[John 14:6]] | The way, the truth, and the life
tile: [[John 15:1]] | The true vine

--- 1. The Bread of Life
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 6:35
lit: The literal hearer | John 6:52
take: Coming is compared to eating; believing to drinking.

--- 2. The Light of the World
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 8:12
rev: Restated | John 12:46
lit: The literal hearer | John 9:40

--- 3. The Door
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 10:9
lit: The hearers | John 10:6
take: One way into the fold. They understood not, so He said it again a different way.

--- 4. The Good Shepherd
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 10:11
rev: The figure points to the cross | John 10:17-18

--- 5. The Resurrection, and the Life
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 11:25-26
lit: The literal hearer, Martha | John 11:24
take: Martha thought of the last day. Jesus pointed her to Himself, the Life standing before her.

--- 6. The Way, the Truth, and the Life
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 14:6
lit: The literal hearer, Thomas | John 14:5
take: Thomas thought of a road. The way is a Person.

--- 7. The True Vine
kicker: The seven “I am” figures
big: John 15:1
rev: Joined to Him | John 15:5
take: Life and fruit come only by being joined to Him.

