=== Device 2: Semitic Parallelism
--- Semitic Parallelism
kicker: Device 2
lead: John often writes the way Hebrew thinkers and poets wrote: by pairing lines.
panel: Synonymous parallelism | He says the same truth twice in slightly different words.
panel: Antithetic parallelism | He sets two opposites next to each other so each one makes the other clearer.
take: The second line is not just decoration. It defines, sharpens, or completes the first.

--- 1. Synonymous Parallelism
kicker: Device 2
lead: John restates the same truth in a second phrasing.
parallel: John 3:15 | John 3:16
row:  | For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son,
row: That whosoever believeth in him | that whosoever believeth in him
row: should not perish, | should not perish,
row: but have eternal life. | but have everlasting life.

--- 1. Synonymous Parallelism, continued
kicker: Device 2
parallel: John 6:40 · Plain statement | John 6:54 · Figured statement
row: every one which seeth the Son, and believeth on him, | Whoso eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood,
row: may have everlasting life. | hath eternal life.
take: Same promise, two different wordings.

--- 2. Antithetic Parallelism
kicker: Device 2
lead: John places two opposites side by side so each defines the other.
parallel: The flesh | The Spirit
row: That which is born of the flesh is flesh; | and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.
row: Flesh produces only flesh. | Spirit produces only spirit.
take: The contrast makes the point unmistakable ([[John 3:6]]).

=== John 1
--- The Word Made Flesh
kicker: John 1
lead: John’s prologue: the eternal Word who is God, made flesh. Then John the Baptist points to Jesus, “Behold the Lamb of God” ([[John 1:29]]), and the first disciples follow: Andrew, Peter, Philip, Nathanael.
big: John 1:1
scripture: John 1:14
take: John declares who Jesus is before any miracle: God come in the flesh.

--- Received Him, Believed on Him
kicker: John 1
fig: Figure | John 1:4-5
lit: Response | John 1:10-11
rev: Meaning | John 1:12-13
take: “Received him” is restated as “believe on his name.” This sets the pattern for the whole book.

=== John 2
--- Destroy This Temple
kicker: John 2
lead: The wedding at Cana, water to wine, His first miracle ([[John 2:11]]). Then up to Jerusalem at Passover, where He drives the money changers out of the temple and the Jews demand a sign ([[John 2:18]]).
fig: Figure | John 2:19
lit: Literal hearer, the Jews | John 2:20

--- Destroy This Temple, continued
kicker: John 2
rev: Meaning | John 2:21-22
take: The first figure given by Jesus in John points to His death and resurrection.

=== John 3
--- Ye Must Be Born Again
kicker: John 3
lead: Nicodemus is a ruler of the Jews, a Pharisee, and he comes to Jesus by night. He is the literal Larry of scripture.
fig: Figure | John 3:3
lit: Literal hearer, Nicodemus | John 3:4

--- Ye Must Be Born Again, continued
kicker: John 3
rev: Meaning | John 3:5-6
take: Parallelism: “born of water” = “born of the flesh” (natural birth); “born of the Spirit” = the second birth.

--- As Moses Lifted Up the Serpent
kicker: John 3
fig: Figure | John 3:14 | Numbers and the brazen serpent: look and live ([[Numbers 21:8-9]]).
rev: Meaning | John 3:15
take: “Look and live” becomes “believe and live.” [[John 3:16]] restates verse 15 and adds to this good news its source: the Father’s love.

--- He That Believeth on the Son
kicker: John 3
rev: 3:18 draws the line at believing alone | John 3:18
rev: John the Baptist repeats this | John 3:36

=== John 4
--- Living Water
kicker: John 4
lead: Passing through Samaria, Jesus sits wearied at Jacob’s well at Sychar about noon. A Samaritan woman comes to draw water while the disciples are off buying food. The chapter ends back in Cana with the nobleman’s son healed ([[John 4:46-54|4:46-54]]).
fig: Figure | John 4:10
lit: Literal hearer, the Samaritan woman | John 4:11-12

--- Living Water, continued
kicker: John 4
rev: Meaning | John 4:13-14
take: Antithetic contrast of two waters. [[John 7:39]] identifies the living water as the Spirit.

--- Meat to Eat
kicker: John 4
fig: Figure | John 4:32
lit: Literal hearers, the disciples | John 4:33
rev: Meaning | John 4:34
take: Even disciples stumble at figures. The work is finished at the cross ([[John 19:30]]).

--- I That Speak unto Thee Am He
kicker: John 4
big: John 4:26
rev: The believers | John 4:41-42
take: The first “I am” in John.

=== John 5
--- Equal with God
kicker: John 5
lead: A feast in Jerusalem. At the pool of Bethesda, Jesus heals a man infirm 38 years: “Rise, take up thy bed, and walk” ([[John 5:8]]). It is the sabbath, and the Jews seek to kill Him.
fig: His words | John 5:17
lit: What the Jews understood | John 5:18
rev: Promise | John 5:24
take: Unbelief did not misunderstand His claim to deity. It rejected it.

=== John 6
--- I Am the Bread of Life
kicker: John 6
lead: Jesus feeds the 5,000 with five barley loaves and two small fishes, then walks on the sea that night. Next day the crowd finds Him in Capernaum wanting more bread. It ends with many disciples walking away ([[John 6:66]]) and Peter: “to whom shall we go?” ([[John 6:68]]).
fig: Figure | John 6:35
lit: Literal hearers, the Jews | John 6:52

--- I Am the Bread of Life, continued
kicker: John 6
rev: Meaning | John 6:63
take: Coming = eating; believing = drinking. The flesh profits nothing.

--- Eating Is Believing
kicker: John 6
parallel: John 6:40 · Plain | John 6:54 · Figure
row: every one which seeth the Son, and believeth on him, | Whoso eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood,
row: may have everlasting life: | hath eternal life;
row: and I will raise him up at the last day. | and I will raise him up at the last day.
take: Same promise, same result: “I will raise him up at the last day.”

--- Eating Is Believing, continued
kicker: John 6
rev: Kept | John 6:37
rev: Kept | John 6:39

=== John 7
--- Whither Will He Go?
kicker: John 7
lead: The feast of tabernacles. His own brethren do not believe; He goes up quietly and teaches in the temple midway through. Officers are sent to take Him. On the last great day of the feast He cries out about living water.
fig: Figure | John 7:33-34
lit: Literal hearers, the Jews | John 7:35-36

--- Whither Will He Go?, continued
kicker: John 7
rev: Meaning | John 16:28
take: They thought of a journey; He spoke of returning to heaven by the cross.

--- Rivers of Living Water
kicker: John 7
fig: Figure | John 7:37-38
rev: John’s explanation | John 7:39
take: Coming and drinking is believing; the rivers that flow from every believer is the Spirit. Not literally water.

=== John 8
--- Believe That I Am He
kicker: John 8
lead: Opens with the woman taken in adultery: “Neither do I condemn thee: go, and sin no more” ([[John 8:11]]). Then in the temple treasury, Jesus declares Himself the light of the world, and a long, rising argument with the Pharisees and Jews follows.
fig: Figure | John 8:21
lit: Literal hearers, the Jews | John 8:22

--- Believe That I Am He, continued
kicker: John 8
rev: Meaning | John 8:23-24
take: Antithesis (Device 2): “Ye are from beneath; I am from above: ye are of this world; I am not of this world” ([[John 8:23]]). They thought He meant to kill Himself; He meant He was going back to where He came from, above. Then believing and His identity are joined in one sentence ([[John 8:24]]).

--- The Truth Shall Make You Free
kicker: John 8
fig: Figure | John 8:31-32
lit: Literal hearers, the Jews which believed on him | John 8:33
rev: Meaning | John 8:34-36
take: They heard ancestry; He meant slavery to sin.

--- Before Abraham Was, I Am
kicker: John 8
fig: Figure | John 8:56
lit: Literal hearers, the Jews | John 8:57
rev: Meaning | John 8:58

--- Before Abraham Was, I Am, continued
kicker: John 8
p: The name of God, [[Exodus 3:14]]. They certainly understood the claim:
scripture: John 8:59

=== John 9
--- Are We Blind Also?
kicker: John 9
lead: Jesus sees a man blind from his birth. He makes clay, anoints his eyes, and sends him to wash in the pool of Siloam, and he comes seeing. It is the sabbath: the Pharisees question the man and his parents and cast him out. Jesus finds him.
fig: Figure | John 9:39
lit: Literal hearers, some of the Pharisees | John 9:40

--- Are We Blind Also?, continued
kicker: John 9
rev: Meaning | John 9:41
take: The blind man received sight; those who claimed to see stayed in darkness.

--- Lord, I Believe
kicker: John 9
scripture: John 9:35-38

--- Lord, I Believe, continued
kicker: John 9
p: Parallelism:
parallel: John 8:12 | John 12:46
row: he that followeth me | whosoever believeth on me
row: shall not walk in darkness | should not abide in darkness

=== John 10
--- I Am the Door
kicker: John 10
lead: Continues straight from chapter 9: Jesus speaks to the same Pharisees who cast out the healed man, the false shepherds. Later, at the feast of dedication in winter, the Jews surround Him in Solomon’s porch: “If thou be the Christ, tell us plainly” ([[John 10:24]]).
fig: Figure | John 10:1-3

--- I Am the Door, continued
kicker: John 10
lit: Hearers, the Pharisees | John 10:6
rev: Meaning | John 10:7; John 10:9
take: “Then said Jesus unto them again” is the dual pattern in John’s own words.

--- I Am the Good Shepherd
kicker: John 10
fig: Figure | John 10:11
rev: Death and resurrection | John 10:17-18
rev: Promise | John 10:28-29
take: The sheep’s security rests on the Shepherd who died and rose.

--- I and My Father Are One
kicker: John 10
fig: His words | John 10:30
lit: What the Jews understood | John 10:33
take: Even His enemies stated the claim plainly. They would not believe it and sought to kill Him.

=== John 11
--- Lazarus Sleepeth
kicker: John 11
lead: Lazarus of Bethany, brother of Mary and Martha, is sick. Jesus stays two more days; by the time He comes, Lazarus has lain in the grave four days. “Jesus wept” ([[John 11:35]]). After He raises him, the council plots His death.
fig: Figure | John 11:11
lit: Literal hearers, the disciples | John 11:12-13

--- Lazarus Sleepeth, continued
kicker: John 11
rev: Meaning | John 11:14
take: For the believer, death is sleep because the Lord will wake him.

--- Believest Thou This?
kicker: John 11
fig: Figure | John 11:23
lit: Literal hearer, Martha | John 11:24
rev: Meaning | John 11:25-26

--- Believest Thou This?, continued
kicker: John 11
rev: Believer’s answer | John 11:27
rev: Proof | John 11:43-44

--- Believest Thou This?, part 3
kicker: John 11
take: Martha held the resurrection as a doctrine for the last day. Jesus pointed her to Himself: the Resurrection was standing in front of her, right then. Then He proved it at the tomb. Even a believer can miss the plain point Jesus reveals.

=== John 12
--- If I Be Lifted Up
kicker: John 12
lead: Six days before Passover, Mary anoints His feet at Bethany. Next day the triumphal entry on a young ass. Certain Greeks ask to see Jesus, and He answers, “The hour is come” ([[John 12:23]]). This chapter closes His public ministry.
fig: Figure | John 12:32
rev: John’s explanation | John 12:33

--- If I Be Lifted Up, continued
kicker: John 12
lit: Literal hearers, the people | John 12:34
take: The third “lifted up.” The first: [[John 3:14]]. The second: [[John 8:28]]. Each time He speaks of the cross, each time He confirms He is the Christ.

=== John 13
--- If I Wash Thee Not
kicker: John 13
lead: The last supper, the night before the cross. Jesus washes the disciples’ feet, points out Judas with the sop, and Judas goes out: “and it was night” ([[John 13:30]]). He foretells Peter’s denial.
fig: Jesus names it a figure | John 13:7
fig: Figure | John 13:8

--- If I Wash Thee Not, continued
kicker: John 13
lit: Literal hearer, Peter | John 13:9
rev: Meaning | John 13:10-11

--- If I Wash Thee Not, part 3
kicker: John 13
take: Peter heard a bath. Jesus meant the cleansing only He gives. The one who is washed (saved) is clean every whit and needs it once; the feet that walk in the world need daily cleansing. Judas had water on his feet but was never washed: he never believed.

--- That Ye May Believe That I Am He
kicker: John 13
big: John 13:19
take: He foretells the betrayal so that its fulfillment proves He is “*I am*” (see the bare “I am” section).

--- That Thou Doest, Do Quickly
kicker: John 13
fig: His words | John 13:27
lit: Literal hearers, the disciples | John 13:28-29

--- That Thou Doest, Do Quickly, continued
kicker: John 13
rev: Meaning | John 13:30
take: The disciples heard an errand. Jesus was sending the betrayer out to set the cross in motion. Judas walked away from the Light, and it was night.

--- Whither I Go
kicker: John 13
fig: Figure | John 13:33
lit: Literal hearer, Peter | John 13:36-37

--- Whither I Go, continued
kicker: John 13
rev: Meaning | John 13:38
take: The same saying He gave the Jews ([[John 7:34]]), now to His own. Peter thought of a place he could walk to; Jesus was going to the cross and to the Father. Peter could not even follow Him past the fire in the courtyard ([[John 18:27]]). This sets up chapter 14: “Let not your heart be troubled.”

=== John 14
--- I Am the Way
kicker: John 14
lead: Still in the upper room. The disciples are troubled: He is leaving, and Peter will deny Him. Jesus comforts them with the Father’s house of many mansions and promises the Comforter.
fig: Figure | John 14:4
lit: Literal hearer, Thomas | John 14:5

--- I Am the Way, continued
kicker: John 14
rev: Meaning | John 14:6
take: The way is a Person, not a road. Dual phrasing in [[John 14:1]]: “Let not your heart be troubled: ye believe in God, believe also in me.”

--- Shew Us the Father
kicker: John 14
fig: Figure | John 14:7
lit: Literal hearer, Philip | John 14:8
rev: Meaning | John 14:9
take: One of the plainest statements of His deity. Promise: “because I live, ye shall live also” ([[John 14:19]]).

=== John 15
--- I Am the True Vine
kicker: John 15
lead: “Arise, let us go hence” ([[John 14:31]]). The teaching continues, likely on the way toward the garden: the vine and branches, love one another, and the world will hate you.
fig: Figure | John 15:1
rev: Meaning | John 15:5

--- I Am the True Vine, continued
kicker: John 15
p: The last of the seven figures. Its theme is fruit, and fruit here is spreading the truth and winning souls:
rev: Fruit | John 15:16
rev: Fruit unto life eternal | John 4:36

--- I Am the True Vine, part 3
kicker: John 15
p: The believer’s eternal security is already given: “him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out” ([[John 6:37]]); “And I give unto them eternal life; and they shall never perish, neither shall any man pluck them out of my hand” ([[John 10:28]]).
p: But the unfruitful servant is taken away:
lit: Taken away | John 15:2
lit: See also | John 15:6

=== John 16
--- A Little While
kicker: John 16
lead: His last teaching before the cross: the Comforter’s work, sorrow turned to joy, and “be of good cheer; I have overcome the world” ([[John 16:33]]).
fig: Figure | John 16:16
lit: Confused hearers, some of His disciples | John 16:17-18

--- A Little While, continued
kicker: John 16
rev: Meaning | John 16:20-22
take: “A little while” was three days: the cross, the grave, the risen Lord.

--- Now Speakest Thou Plainly
kicker: John 16
scripture: John 16:25-30 | mark: by this we believe

--- Now Speakest Thou Plainly, continued
kicker: John 16
take: Here Jesus names the very method this study follows. He has been speaking in proverbs, figures with a spiritual meaning, and now He says it plainly: He came from the Father and goes back to the Father. The disciples hear the plain word and answer the way every figure in John aims: “by this we believe.” Reading His figures as figures, and looking for the plain meaning He gives, is following Jesus’ own description of how He taught.

=== John 17
--- Keep Through Thine Own Name
kicker: John 17
lead: The prayer of Jesus the Son to God the Father on behalf of the apostles, the night before the cross.
scripture: John 17:12-16

--- Keep Through Thine Own Name, continued
kicker: John 17
take: Jesus spoke in the world and gave the apostles the word of God. The world hated that word, and hated them for it. But the apostles heard it and believed, because they are not of the world.

=== John 18
--- I Am He
kicker: John 18
lead: Over the brook Cedron into the garden. Judas comes with a band of men and officers, and Jesus is arrested; Peter cuts off Malchus’s ear. Trials before Annas, Caiaphas, and Pilate; Peter denies Him three times; the crowd chooses Barabbas.
scripture: John 18:6 | mark: I am he
scripture: John 18:8 | mark: I am he

--- I Am He, continued
kicker: John 18
take: “*I am he*” is more than Jesus singling Himself out to the guards. It is the name of God ([[Exodus 3:14]]), His declaration that He is the Christ, and the truth of that Word knocks armed men to the ground. He is not taken by force; He lays down His life: “No man taketh it from me, but I lay it down of myself. I have power to lay it down, and I have power to take it again. This commandment have I received of my Father” ([[John 10:18]]).

=== John 19
--- It Is Finished
kicker: John 19
lead: Scourged and crowned with thorns: “Behold the man!” ([[John 19:5]]). Crucified at Golgotha between two others under the title JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS. He commits His mother to John, dies, His side is pierced, and Joseph of Arimathaea and Nicodemus bury Him.
big: John 19:30

--- It Is Finished, continued
kicker: John 19
p: Every figure pointed here: temple, serpent, bread, shepherd. The work is complete: “My meat is to do the will of him that sent me, and to finish his work” ([[John 4:34]]); “I have glorified thee on the earth: I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do” ([[John 17:4]]). Nothing is left for the sinner to add:
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:3-4

=== John 20
--- My Lord and My God
kicker: John 20
lead: Early on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene finds the stone taken away. Peter and John run to the empty tomb. Jesus appears to Mary, then to the disciples behind shut doors. Thomas is absent; eight days later, he is there.
lit: Literal demand, Thomas called Didymus | John 20:24-25

--- My Lord and My God, continued
kicker: John 20
rev: Jesus appears and offers Thomas the literal | John 20:26-28

--- My Lord and My God, part 3
kicker: John 20
rev: Said again, for us | John 20:29
big: John 20:30-31
take: Thomas’s confession matches [[John 1:1]]: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.” Verse 31 states the purpose of the whole book.

=== John 21
--- What Is That to Thee?
kicker: John 21
lead: Overwhelmed, Peter leaves the ministry for his old life: “Simon Peter saith unto them, I go a fishing. They say unto him, We also go with thee” ([[John 21:3]]). Six others join him at the sea of Tiberias, and they fish all night and catch nothing. Jesus appears on the shore, fills the net with 153 fish, and serves them breakfast.

--- What Is That to Thee?, continued
kicker: John 21
lead: Then three times He asks Peter, “lovest thou me?”, giving him the chance to answer each of his three denials. He foretells Peter’s martyrdom, Peter asks about John, and the book closes with one final misunderstanding.
fig: His words | John 21:22
lit: Literal hearers | John 21:23

--- What Is That to Thee?, part 3
kicker: John 21
center: John corrects the rumor by repeating exactly what Jesus said. Read what the Lord said, not read something into it.
read: john-6-bread-of-life | The Bread of Life: John 6
